WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:24.545
&gt;&gt; Hi, I'm Dushyanth Narayanan,

00:00:24.546 --> 00:00:25.640
I'm a researcher at

00:00:25.641 --> 00:00:28.145
Microsoft Research in
Cambridge in the UK,

00:00:28.146 --> 00:00:30.994
and I'm going to tell
you about FaRM and A1,

00:00:30.995 --> 00:00:32.390
which me and my team have been

00:00:32.391 --> 00:00:34.500
working on over
the last five years.

00:00:34.501 --> 00:00:37.910
If we look at distributed
computing platforms today,

00:00:37.911 --> 00:00:41.845
we have good platforms for
batch computation at scale,

00:00:41.846 --> 00:00:43.709
such as Cosmos or MapReduce,

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:45.919
which can run large computations

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:47.175
over thousands of machines,

00:00:47.176 --> 00:00:48.650
for minutes to hours.

00:00:48.651 --> 00:00:53.455
We also have good platforms
that can provide low latency,

00:00:53.456 --> 00:00:55.290
but they don't scale out,

00:00:55.291 --> 00:00:57.140
and FaRM is a platform for

00:00:57.141 --> 00:01:00.100
providing low latency at scale.

00:01:00.101 --> 00:01:03.830
FaRM makes it easy to build
low latency scale out

00:01:03.831 --> 00:01:06.290
applications by hiding the things

00:01:06.291 --> 00:01:08.665
that make distributed
applications hard,

00:01:08.666 --> 00:01:13.235
which is concurrency, failures,
and data distribution.

00:01:13.236 --> 00:01:15.470
So, FaRM abstracts this away and

00:01:15.471 --> 00:01:17.620
provides the programmer with

00:01:17.621 --> 00:01:20.030
the abstraction of
a single machine

00:01:20.031 --> 00:01:23.090
that pulls the resources
of the entire cluster,

00:01:23.091 --> 00:01:28.010
runs transactions one at
a time, and doesn't fail.

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:30.320
The way we do this is

00:01:30.321 --> 00:01:32.990
by providing
distributed transactions

00:01:32.991 --> 00:01:35.240
with strict
serializability which has

00:01:35.241 --> 00:01:38.430
very strong consistency,
and high availability.

00:01:38.431 --> 00:01:41.565
Distributed transactions
are a very old idea,

00:01:41.566 --> 00:01:44.890
but in the past, people
thought they wouldn't scale,

00:01:44.891 --> 00:01:46.860
and that they wouldn't
perform well.

00:01:46.861 --> 00:01:50.140
In FaRM, we showed that
within the data center,

00:01:50.141 --> 00:01:52.305
we can have distributed
transactions,

00:01:52.306 --> 00:01:54.225
with very strong consistency,

00:01:54.226 --> 00:01:57.545
high availability, and
great performance.

00:01:57.546 --> 00:02:00.424
To achieve this, we leverage

00:02:00.425 --> 00:02:01.775
the hardware that's present

00:02:01.776 --> 00:02:03.575
in our Azure data centers today.

00:02:03.576 --> 00:02:06.710
So, plentiful and cheap DRAM
means we can keep

00:02:06.711 --> 00:02:08.285
the working set of applications

00:02:08.286 --> 00:02:10.080
in memory for low latency.

00:02:10.081 --> 00:02:13.045
Lithium ion batteries
integrated into racks,

00:02:13.046 --> 00:02:16.605
let us make this memory
non-volatile at low cost.

00:02:16.606 --> 00:02:21.390
Fast RDMA networking, let us
cut the cost of scale out.

00:02:21.391 --> 00:02:24.740
Now, the hardware alone doesn't
give us this performance.

00:02:24.741 --> 00:02:28.370
So, there was
a huge amount of work to

00:02:28.371 --> 00:02:30.860
get the performance out
of the hardware which

00:02:30.861 --> 00:02:33.950
involved redesigning
the communication stack,

00:02:33.951 --> 00:02:36.500
and new distributed
systems protocols

00:02:36.501 --> 00:02:39.255
such as a novel transaction
commit protocol.

00:02:39.256 --> 00:02:40.890
A result of this work,

00:02:40.891 --> 00:02:43.380
we have amazing scalability,

00:02:43.381 --> 00:02:45.540
high throughput and low latency,

00:02:45.541 --> 00:02:47.420
and extremely high availability.

00:02:47.421 --> 00:02:48.650
So, for an example, we can

00:02:48.651 --> 00:02:50.390
recover to full throughput from

00:02:50.391 --> 00:02:54.655
a failure in 67 milliseconds
in this experiment,

00:02:54.656 --> 00:02:56.690
which is less time than

00:02:56.691 --> 00:02:58.730
some systems take to commit

00:02:58.731 --> 00:03:01.965
a single transaction even when
they don't have failures.

00:03:01.966 --> 00:03:04.630
So, FaRM, as a research idea

00:03:04.631 --> 00:03:07.130
started about
five years ago, right?

00:03:07.131 --> 00:03:09.390
We did foundational research on

00:03:09.391 --> 00:03:12.615
FaRM over the next two years,

00:03:12.616 --> 00:03:14.470
and over the last three years,

00:03:14.471 --> 00:03:16.265
we've been pushing
FaRM to production

00:03:16.266 --> 00:03:17.960
as well as building A1,

00:03:17.961 --> 00:03:19.730
a graph database that

00:03:19.731 --> 00:03:21.320
we're exposing to
internal customers in

00:03:21.321 --> 00:03:23.750
Microsoft because there
was a huge need for

00:03:23.751 --> 00:03:26.210
a good graph platform
with high performance,

00:03:26.211 --> 00:03:28.170
scalability and
high availability.

00:03:28.171 --> 00:03:31.040
So, where we are now is
that we've just gone

00:03:31.041 --> 00:03:34.640
live with our first scenario
for this graph platform.

00:03:34.641 --> 00:03:37.850
My colleague Alex is
going to talk about that.

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:42.470
&gt;&gt; Hi, my name is Alex Shamis,

00:03:42.471 --> 00:03:46.230
and I work with Dushyanth and
a bunch of other people to

00:03:46.231 --> 00:03:50.240
build FaRM and then
our partner product called A1.

00:03:50.241 --> 00:03:52.970
A1 is a distributed
graph database that

00:03:52.971 --> 00:03:55.864
we've built to help accelerate

00:03:55.865 --> 00:03:59.090
applications that need
to use graphs and have

00:03:59.091 --> 00:04:00.650
to have consistent graph

00:04:00.651 --> 00:04:02.840
but are too big to
fit one application.

00:04:02.841 --> 00:04:06.230
I'll show an example of
what we've been able to do,

00:04:06.231 --> 00:04:08.330
and what we've been able to
put into production using

00:04:08.331 --> 00:04:09.420
the technologies that Dushyanth

00:04:09.421 --> 00:04:10.850
just talked about in FaRM,

00:04:10.851 --> 00:04:12.650
along with all the other
people that worked

00:04:12.651 --> 00:04:15.435
on FaRM and the A1 product.

00:04:15.436 --> 00:04:18.635
So, using a Bing query,

00:04:18.636 --> 00:04:22.970
there was recently a news article

00:04:22.971 --> 00:04:24.890
saying that Russell Wilson is

00:04:24.891 --> 00:04:28.095
the most sacked football
player in the NFL.

00:04:28.096 --> 00:04:30.065
So, an interesting
question would be,

00:04:30.066 --> 00:04:32.250
so who who sacks Russell Wilson

00:04:32.251 --> 00:04:34.690
so much and who are
the statistics on this?

00:04:34.691 --> 00:04:36.285
So, we can type into Bing,

00:04:36.286 --> 00:04:39.090
who sacked Russell
Wilson the most?

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:41.750
Then in seconds, we
get the results.

00:04:41.751 --> 00:04:43.565
We get this result,

00:04:43.566 --> 00:04:46.305
showing that the people that have

00:04:46.306 --> 00:04:49.090
sacked Russell Wilson the
most is Robert Quinn.

00:04:49.091 --> 00:04:53.585
This query, once it
comes to our back end,

00:04:53.586 --> 00:04:58.340
executes a six hop graph query
that does 22,000 reads,

00:04:58.341 --> 00:05:00.970
and were able to perform this
in tens of milliseconds.

00:05:00.971 --> 00:05:03.635
So, this is just a small example

00:05:03.636 --> 00:05:05.795
of what we would be able
to do with FaRM and A1,

00:05:05.796 --> 00:05:09.390
and put into production
with Bing. Thank you.

